WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.060
Serialization code needs tests that go beyond well-formed examples.

2
00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:22.040
Useful tests include missing fields, unknown fields, duplicated fields, invalid types, boundary lengths, maximum sizes, empty values, nested data, truncated data, extra data, incompatible versions, invalid encoding, and failed integrity checks.

3
00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:27.260
These cases show whether the parser fails safely or accepts data it should reject.

4
00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:30.980
Compatibility tests protect format evolution.

5
00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:37.960
If the product promises that old data can be read by new code, that promise should be exercised with real baseline examples.

6
00:00:38.060 --> 00:00:42.620
If new data must fail safely in old readers, that behavior should be intentional.

7
00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:49.280
Compatibility tests turn a vague support statement into evidence that reviewers can inspect.

8
00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:59.640
Defensive fuzzing can help reveal parser crashes, hangs, memory errors, excessive resource use, and unexpected acceptance of malformed inputs.

9
00:00:59.740 --> 00:01:11.340
The important point is safe engineering practice: run fuzzing in controlled test environments, track findings as defects, and connect results back to parser limits and validation rules.

10
00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:15.120
Fuzzing complements design review; it does not replace it.

11
00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:18.840
Logs should not expose sensitive serialized data.

12
00:01:18.940 --> 00:01:27.660
Code review should check trust boundaries, size limits, version rules, object construction, cryptographic assumptions, redaction, and failure behavior.

13
00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:38.080
A good review asks what happens when the input is old, partial, oversized, inconsistent, or validly structured but not authorized for the requested operation.

14
00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:42.440
Findings from tests and fuzzing should feed back into the format contract.

15
00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:51.260
If a malformed case surprises the parser, document the expected behavior, add a regression test, and decide whether the schema or limits need to be clearer.
